--TEST--
Every `Core\Os` member answers under a deployment that grants nothing, because a fact about the process asking is not a reach into anything — `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door` and `rule:core-api/tier-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The `nvs.toml` beside this file grants nothing at all, which is the state
// every capability is in by default. `Core\IO::read` under this file would be
// refused at the door; every member here answers, because a pid, a hostname, a
// core count and this process's own memory are facts about the process doing
// the asking and reach nothing it does not already inhabit. That is why
// `Core\Os` has no row in the registry's capability table rather than five rows
// saying `None`.
//
// Written as one branch per member rather than as an echo of each answer,
// because a refusal is a throw and not a value: what this pins is that the
// call returns at all.
if (Core\Os::pid() > 0) {
    echo "pid: answered\n";
} else {
    echo "pid: no number\n";
}

if (Core\Str::length(Core\Os::hostname()) > 0) {
    echo "hostname: answered\n";
} else {
    echo "hostname: no name\n";
}

if (Core\Os::cpuCount() > 0) {
    echo "cpuCount: answered\n";
} else {
    echo "cpuCount: no count\n";
}

if (Core\Os::residentBytes() > 0) {
    echo "residentBytes: answered\n";
} else {
    echo "residentBytes: no figure\n";
}

// The one member that can refuse, and the refusal has to be about the
// *platform* rather than about a grant: a capability refusal would name the
// capability, and this branch prints the message when it is anything but the
// sentence Windows gets.
try {
    var $load = Core\Os::loadAverage();
    if (Core\Arr::count($load) == 3) {
        echo "loadAverage: answered\n";
    } else {
        echo "loadAverage: the wrong number of figures\n";
    }
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($refused->message, "keeps no load average")) {
        echo "loadAverage: answered\n";
    } else {
        echo "loadAverage: ", $refused->message, "\n";
    }
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Deliberately empty of grants. A block with nothing in it is how an operator
# writes "this program reaches nothing", and every member above still answers
# under it.
[capabilities]
--EXPECT--
pid: answered
hostname: answered
cpuCount: answered
residentBytes: answered
loadAverage: answered
